the \emph{differential Yang--Baxter relation} (sometimes also called the Sutherland relation, 
cf.~\cite{Sutherland70,Thacker86,KPS13,IZ14}),
\begin{equation}
\big[h^{n|m},\bL(z) \stimes \bL(z)\big] = \bL(z)\stimes \bL^{\prime}(z) - \bL^{\prime}(z)\stimes \bL(z),
\label{eqn:Sutherland}
\end{equation}
where $\bL^{\prime}(z)\equiv \partial_{z}\bL(z)$. In the above derivation we have employed the fact that
$\mathfrak{su}(n|m)$-symmetric Hamiltonian densities $h^{n|m}$ coincide with graded permutations $P^{n|m}$ acting on
$\mathbb{C}^{n|m}\otimes \mathbb{C}^{n|m}$, that is
\begin{equation}
h^{n|m} = P^{n|m}\partial_{z} R^{n|m}(z).
\label{eqn:hamiltonian_density}
\end{equation}
For the so-called fundamental lattice models, relation \eqref{eqn:Sutherland} is in fact equivalent to the zero-curvature property of
the Lax connection.\footnote{Relation \eqref{eqn:Sutherland} should not be confused with the lattice version of the
Lax representation which takes the local form
$\partial_{t}\bL_{i}(z)=\ii[H,\bL_{i}(z)]=\mathbf{A}_{i+1}(z)\bL_{i}(z)-\bL_{i}(z)\mathbf{A}_{i}(z)$, with matrices 
$\bL_{i}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{i}$ are identified with spatial and temporal components of the (discrete) connection associated
to the auxiliary linear problem.}
More importantly, the differential Yang--Baxter relation \eqref{eqn:Sutherland_general} is satisfied on
a purely \emph{algebraic} level, i.e. irrespective of a representations of the auxiliary components of the $\bL$-operator.

